.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt GREP 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm grep ,
.Nm egrep ,
.Nm fgrep
.Nd print lines that match patterns
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm grep
.Op Fl abcEFGHhIiLlnOopqRrSsUvwxy
.Op Fl A Ar num
.Op Fl B Ar num
.Op Fl C Ar num
.Op Fl d Ar action
.Op Fl e Ar pattern
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl m Ar num
.Op Fl - Ns Cm color Ns Op = Ns Ar when
.Op Fl - Ns Cm exclude Ns = Ns Ar glob
.Op Fl - Ns Cm exclude-dir Ns = Ns Ar glob
.Op Fl - Ns Cm include Ns = Ns Ar glob
.Op Fl - Ns Cm line-buffered
.Op Fl - Ns Cm null
.Op Ar pattern
.Op Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
searches each
.Ar file
for lines that match any of the patterns, and writes them to the standard
output.
A
.Ar file
of
.Sq Fl ,
or none at all, means the standard input
.Pq or, with Fl r , the working directory .
.Pp
The patterns are those given with
.Fl e
and
.Fl f ,
or else the first operand.
A pattern containing newlines is one pattern per line.
The null pattern matches every line.
Patterns are basic regular expressions unless
.Fl E
or
.Fl F
is given; see
.Xr re_format 7 .
.Nm
works on bytes: a line is everything up to a newline, and the last line of a
file needs none.
.Pp
.Nm egrep
is
.Nm grep Fl E
and
.Nm fgrep
is
.Nm grep Fl F ;
they are links to the same program.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl A Ar num
Print
.Ar num
lines of context after each selected line.
.It Fl a
Treat binary files as text.
.It Fl B Ar num
Print
.Ar num
lines of context before each selected line.
.It Fl b
Precede each line (or, with
.Fl o ,
each match) with its byte offset in the file.
.It Fl C Ar num , Fl Ar num
Print
.Ar num
lines of context before and after each selected line.
Groups of lines that are not adjacent are separated by a line of
.Ql -- .
.It Fl c
Write only the number of selected lines in each file.
.It Fl d Ar action
What to do with a directory operand:
.Cm read
(the default; it fails, as reading a directory does),
.Cm skip ,
or
.Cm recurse
(the same as
.Fl r ) .
.It Fl E
Patterns are extended regular expressions.
.It Fl e Ar pattern
Use
.Ar pattern .
Can be given more than once.
.It Fl F
Patterns are fixed strings.
.It Fl f Ar file
Read patterns from
.Ar file ,
one per line.
An empty
.Ar file
gives no patterns, matching nothing.
.It Fl G
Patterns are basic regular expressions (the default).
.It Fl H
Always precede lines with the file name.
.It Fl h
Never precede lines with the file name.
.It Fl I
Treat binary files as not matching.
.It Fl i , Fl y
Ignore the case of letters.
.It Fl L
Write only the names of files with no selected line.
.It Fl l
Write only the names of files with a selected line.
.It Fl m Ar num
Stop reading a file after
.Ar num
selected lines.
.It Fl n
Precede each line with its line number.
.It Fl O
With
.Fl r ,
follow symbolic links named on the command line only.
.It Fl o
Print only the parts of lines that match, each on its own line.
.It Fl p
With
.Fl r ,
follow no symbolic links found in a directory (the default).
.It Fl q
Write nothing; exit with status 0 at the first selected line.
.It Fl R , Fl r
Search directories recursively.
.It Fl S
With
.Fl r ,
follow all symbolic links.
.It Fl s
Write no messages about missing or unreadable files.
.It Fl U
Accepted for compatibility.
.It Fl v
Select the lines that do not match.
.It Fl w
Match only whole words: the match must be at the start of the line or after a
character that is not a letter, digit or underscore, and likewise at its end.
.It Fl x
Match only whole lines.
.It Fl - Ns Cm binary-files Ns = Ns Ar type
.Cm binary
(the default),
.Cm text
(as
.Fl a ) ,
or
.Cm without-match
(as
.Fl I ) .
.It Fl - Ns Cm color Ns Op = Ns Ar when
Highlight matches:
.Cm auto
(the default) when the standard output is a terminal,
.Cm always
or
.Cm never .
.It Fl - Ns Cm context Ns Op = Ns Ar num
The same as
.Fl C ,
2 lines by default.
.It Fl - Ns Cm exclude Ns = Ns Ar glob
In a recursive search, skip files whose name matches
.Ar glob .
.It Fl - Ns Cm exclude-dir Ns = Ns Ar glob
In a recursive search, skip directories whose name matches
.Ar glob .
.It Fl - Ns Cm include Ns = Ns Ar glob
In a recursive search, read only files whose name matches
.Ar glob
(and no
.Fl - Ns Cm exclude ) .
.It Fl - Ns Cm line-buffered
Flush the output after every line.
.It Fl - Ns Cm null
Follow each file name with a NUL byte instead of
.Ql \&:
or a newline.
.El
.Pp
The file name precedes each line when more than one file is searched or the
search is recursive.
Selected lines are separated from their prefixes by
.Ql \&: ,
context lines by
.Ql - .
.Pp
A file is binary if its first 32 KiB contain a NUL byte.
A match in a binary file prints
.Dq Binary file Ar name Li matches
in place of the line.
.Pp
In a recursive search, devices, FIFOs and sockets are skipped, and the names in
a directory are read in sorted order.
.Pp
Options may follow the operands, as with the other BSD
.Nm
implementations;
.Ql --
ends them.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width GREP_COLORS
.It Ev GREP_COLOR
The SGR sequence for highlighted matches; the default is
.Ql 01;31 .
.It Ev GREP_COLORS
If it contains an
.Cm mt=
or
.Cm ms=
field, that sequence is used instead.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
A line was selected.
.It 1
No line was selected.
.It 2
An error occurred.
With
.Fl q ,
a selected line still gives 0.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Find a function in the sources of a tree:
.Dl $ grep -rn --include='*.rs' 'fn main' sys
.Pp
Lines that are not comments or empty:
.Dl $ grep -Ev '^(#|$)' /etc/rc.conf
.Pp
Whether a user exists:
.Dl $ grep -q '^alice:' /etc/passwd && echo yes
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ed 1 ,
.Xr sed 1 ,
.Xr re_format 7
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq), whose options are
.Fl c , E , e , F , f , i , l , n , q , s , v , w
and
.Fl x .
The others are those of the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v4 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
.Sh BUGS
With
.Fl L ,
the exit status reflects whether any line was selected, not whether a file was
named (the BSD convention).
